# Clip interval invariant

Every stored `GameTag` must satisfy `startMs >= 0`, `endMs >= startMs`, and `startMs <= markMs <= endMs`. Flooring `startMs` at 0 (lead time before t=0) or shrinking `endMs` can push `markMs` outside that range. Clamp `markMs` into the interval on **every** write path, not only `importTags()`.

```cpp
// BAD — addTag seeds start/end from mark, clamps start to 0, leaves mark unchanged
qint64 start = stored.markMs - duration.leadMs;
if (start < 0) start = 0;
stored.startMs = start;
stored.endMs = stored.markMs + duration.lagMs;
// markMs can still be < startMs (negative mark) or > endMs after later end clamps

// GOOD — same clamp helper as importTags after seed or explicit interval
stored.startMs = stored.markMs - duration.leadMs;
stored.endMs = stored.markMs + duration.lagMs;
clampTagInterval(stored);  // start>=0, end>=start, mark inside [start, end]
```

Do not duplicate clamp logic in `addTag` and `importTags`. One helper, both callers.

`setTagInterval` sets `intervalManuallyEdited` **only after** start/end actually change. A clamped no-op must not lock the tag; `applyDefaultsToUntrimmedTags` skips only truly trimmed clips.

```cpp
// BAD — lock even when clamping leaves the stored interval unchanged
tag.intervalManuallyEdited = true;
if (tag.startMs == startMs && tag.endMs == endMs) return true;

// GOOD — lock only on a real write
if (tag.startMs == startMs && tag.endMs == endMs) return true;
tag.startMs = startMs;
tag.endMs = endMs;
tag.intervalManuallyEdited = true;
clampTagInterval(tag);  // keep markMs inside the new interval
```
